  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence I,  the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence II, the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence III, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{n}-3)}{(1+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{1+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence I diverges.",
